🔹 Part 1: Transport Layer Protocols – TCP Basics
Q1. What are the two main transport layer protocols used on the Internet?

TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol).

Q2. Why is TCP called a reliable protocol?

It guarantees delivery of data packets to the correct destination.

The receiver sends an acknowledgement (ACK) to the sender.

It uses retransmission to compensate for packet loss.

Q3. What does “connection-oriented” mean in TCP?

TCP establishes an end-to-end connection between source and destination before exchanging data.

The connection is maintained until the application programs at both ends finish exchanging data.

Q4. How does TCP handle congestion and flow control?

By controlling the window size.

TCP reacts to congestion by reducing the sender window size.

Q5. What is meant by “in-order delivery” in TCP?

TCP ensures data packets are delivered to the destination in the same order they were sent.

Sequence numbers are used to coordinate which data has been transmitted and received.

Q6. What does “full duplex” mean for a TCP connection?

It allows data to be sent in both directions at the same time.

Q7. How is a TCP connection uniquely identified?

By the combination of port numbers and IP addresses of sender and receiver.

IP addresses indicate which systems are communicating.

Port numbers indicate which end-to-end sockets are communicating.

Q8. What is the relationship between a TCP segment and an IP datagram?

TCP segments are encapsulated into an IP datagram.

The TCP header immediately follows the IP header during transmission.

TCP segment = TCP header + Data chunk

Q9. What kind of protocol combination is TCP?

TCP uses a combination of Selective Repeat (SR) and Go-Back-N protocols.

Specifically: 75% SR protocol and 25% Go-Back-N protocol.

It can use both selective and cumulative acknowledgements.

Q10. What happens when a receiver gets an out-of-order packet in TCP?

The receiver accepts the out-of-order packet but sends an acknowledgement for the expected packet.

The receiver may send independent acknowledgements or cumulative acknowledgements.

Q11. Is TCP a byte stream protocol? Explain.

Yes. The application layer sends data to the transport layer without any limitation.

TCP divides the data into chunks (each chunk is a collection of bytes).

It then creates TCP segments by adding a TCP header to each data chunk.

Q12. What error checking and recovery mechanisms does TCP provide?

Checksum

Acknowledgement

Retransmission

🔹 Part 2: TCP Header – Field-by-Field (High-Yield Viva Area)
Q13. What is the size of the TCP header? What are the minimum and maximum lengths?

Minimum: 20 bytes (5 rows × 4 bytes).

Maximum: 60 bytes (20 bytes + 40 bytes of options).

Range: [20 bytes, 60 bytes].

Q14. What is the size of the Source Port and Destination Port fields?

Both are 16-bit fields.

Source Port identifies the sending application’s port.

Destination Port identifies the receiving application’s port.

Q15. What is the Sequence Number field? What does it contain?

32-bit field.

TCP assigns a unique sequence number to each byte of data in the TCP segment.

This field contains the sequence number of the first data byte.

Q16. What is the Acknowledgement Number field?

32-bit field.

Contains the sequence number of the data byte the receiver expects to receive next from the sender.

It is always the sequence number of the last received data byte + 1.

Q17. What is the Header Length field? How is it interpreted?

4-bit field.

Contains the length of the TCP header.

Helps know where the actual data begins.

Scaling factor = 4: Header length = Header length field value × 4 bytes.

Example: Field value 5 → 5 × 4 = 20 bytes; Field value 15 → 15 × 4 = 60 bytes.

Q18. Why is there a scaling factor of 4 for the Header Length field?

The field is only 4 bits, so it can represent decimal values [0, 15].

But actual header length range is [20, 60] bytes.

So, the field value is multiplied by 4 to represent the actual length.

Q19. What is the difference between “Header length” and “Header length field value”?

Header length field value range: [5, 15].

Header length (actual) range: [20, 60] bytes.

Q20. What are the Reserved Bits?

6 bits that are not used (reserved).

Q21. Explain the URG bit and Urgent Pointer.

URG bit = 1 → indicates that certain data in the current segment is urgent.

Urgent data is pointed out by the Urgent Pointer field.

Urgent data is prioritized and forwarded to the receiving application on a separate channel.

Urgent Pointer is a 16-bit field; valid only if URG = 1.

Formulas:

Number of urgent bytes = Urgent pointer + 1

End of urgent byte = Sequence number of first byte in segment + Urgent pointer

Q22. Explain the ACK bit.

Indicates whether the Acknowledgement Number field is valid.

ACK = 1 → Acknowledgement number is valid.

For all TCP segments except the request segment, ACK bit is set to 1.

The request segment (sent during Three-Way Handshake) has ACK = 0.

Q23. Explain the PSH bit.

PSH = 1 → push the entire buffer immediately to the receiving application.

No wait for filling the entire buffer.

Unlike URG, PSH does not prioritize data.

Order is maintained as segments arrived.

Not a good practice to set PSH = 1 because it disrupts the receiver’s CPU and forces immediate action.

Q24. Explain the RST bit.

RST = 1 → reset the TCP connection.

Indicates the receiver to terminate the connection immediately.

Both sides release the connection and all resources abnormally.

Data transfer ceases in both directions.

May result in loss of data in transit.

Used only for unrecoverable errors or when normal termination is impossible.

Q25. Explain the SYN bit.

SYN = 1 → synchronize sequence numbers.

Indicates the receiver that the sequence number in the TCP header is the initial sequence number.

The request segment in the Three-Way Handshake has SYN = 1.

Q26. Explain the FIN bit.

FIN = 1 → the sender wants to terminate the connection.

The FIN segment sent for TCP connection termination contains FIN = 1.

Q27. What is the Window Size field?

16-bit field.

Contains the size of the receiving window of the sender.

Advertises how much data (in bytes) the sender can receive without acknowledgement.

Used for Flow Control.

Changes dynamically during transmission: usually increases up to a point where congestion is detected, then reduces to avoid dropping packets.

Q28. What is the Checksum field?

16-bit field used for error control.

Verifies the integrity of data in the TCP payload.

Sender adds CRC checksum before sending; receiver rejects data that fails the CRC check.

🔹 Part 3: TCP Options Field (Frequently Asked)
Q29. What is the size of the Options field? What is it used for?

Size: 0 to 40 bytes.

Used for:

Time stamp

Window size extension

Parameter negotiation

Padding

Q30. How is the Time Stamp option used?

Multiple segments with the same sequence number may appear at the receiver.

Time stamp marks the age of TCP segments.

Receiver uses it to identify the correct segment.

Q31. How is Window Size Extension achieved?

The Window Size field is only 16 bits.

To represent a window size greater than 16 bits, the Options field is used.

The extra bits are appended in the Options field.

Q32. What is Parameter Negotiation in the Options field?

During connection establishment, both sender and receiver must specify their Maximum Segment Size (MSS).

There is no special field for MSS, so it is specified using the Options field.

Q33. What is Padding? Why is it needed?

Adding dummy data to fill unused space and make the header conform to standard size.

When header length is not a multiple of 4, extra zeroes are padded in the Options field.

Example: Header length = 30 bytes → add 2 bytes of dummy data → 32 bytes → 32 / 4 = 8 (header length field value).

Worst case: 3 bytes of dummy data may be needed.

🔹 Part 4: Sequence Number & Wrap Around Time (From Lecture 31)
Note: Lecture 31 PDF content was mostly title/blank pages in the file you shared. Only the title gives a hint: “TCP Sequence Number & Wrap Around Time.”
Based on standard TCP theory (and what examiners ask from this topic), here are the likely viva questions:

Q34. What is the range of TCP sequence numbers?

Sequence number field is 32 bits → range is 0 to 2³² − 1 (0 to 4,294,967,295).

Q35. What is “Wrap Around Time” in TCP?

The time taken for the sequence number to wrap around from the maximum value back to 0.

Wrap Around Time = (2³²) / (Data Rate in bytes per second).

Q36. Why is Wrap Around Time important?

It determines how long a TCP connection can remain active before old segments could be confused with new ones.

It is related to the Maximum Segment Lifetime (MSL) and TIME_WAIT state.

Q37. What is the initial sequence number (ISN)? How is it chosen?

The first sequence number used in a TCP connection.

Chosen randomly (to prevent old duplicate segments from being accepted) and synchronized during the Three-Way Handshake using the SYN bit.

🔹 Part 5: Tricky Viva Traps (Examiners Love These)
Trap Question	Correct Answer
“Is TCP 100% Selective Repeat?”	No — it’s a combination of 75% SR and 25% Go-Back-N.
“Does PSH prioritize data like URG?”	No. PSH only pushes the buffer immediately; URG prioritizes.
“Is ACK bit always 1?”	No. It is 0 in the request segment (SYN segment) during the Three-Way Handshake.
“What is the header length if the field value is 20?”	Invalid — max field value is 15 (since 4 bits). Header length = 15 × 4 = 60 bytes.
“Can the TCP header be 64 bytes?”	No. Maximum is 60 bytes (20 + 40 options).
“Does the receiver reject out-of-order packets in TCP?”	No. It accepts them but sends ACK for the expected packet.
“What is the difference between flow control and congestion control?”	Flow control prevents the sender from overwhelming the receiver; congestion control prevents overwhelming the network. TCP handles both via window size.
