🔹 Lecture 31: TCP Sequence Number & Wrap Around Time — Viva Q&A
Q1. What is the size of the TCP sequence number field?

32 bits.

Range: 0 to 2³² − 1 (0 to 4,294,967,295).

Q2. What does the sequence number represent in TCP?

TCP is a byte-stream protocol.

The sequence number is assigned to each byte of data.

The Sequence Number field in the header contains the sequence number of the first data byte in that segment.

Q3. If a TCP segment carries 1000 bytes of data and the sequence number is 5000, what is the sequence number of the next segment?

Next sequence number = 5000 + 1000 = 6000.

(Assuming no SYN/FIN flags consume sequence numbers.)

Q4. Do SYN and FIN consume sequence numbers?

Yes. SYN and FIN each consume one sequence number.

Example: If SYN has sequence number X, the first data byte will have sequence number X + 1.

Q5. What is the initial sequence number (ISN)?

The first sequence number used in a TCP connection.

It is chosen randomly (not necessarily 0) to prevent old duplicate segments from being accepted.

Synchronized during the Three-Way Handshake using the SYN bit.

Q6. What is “Wrap Around” in TCP?

Since the sequence number field is 32 bits, after reaching 2³² − 1, the next sequence number wraps around to 0.

This is called sequence number wrap around.

Q7. What is Wrap Around Time (WAT)?

The time taken for the sequence number space to completely wrap around from 0 to 2³² − 1 and back to 0.

