Q9. Why is Wrap Around Time important?

It determines the maximum lifetime of a TCP connection before old segments could be confused with new ones.

It is related to Maximum Segment Lifetime (MSL) and the TIME_WAIT state.

If a connection lasts longer than WAT, old duplicate segments might still be in the network and cause confusion.

Q10. What is Maximum Segment Lifetime (MSL)?

The maximum time a TCP segment can exist in the network before being discarded.

Typically 2 minutes (implementation-dependent).

The TIME_WAIT state lasts 2 × MSL to ensure old segments are drained.

Q11. What is the relationship between WAT, MSL, and the 32-bit sequence number?

For correct operation: Wrap Around Time > 2 × MSL (or at least > MSL).

If WAT is too small (very high data rate), old segments could wrap around and be mistaken for new ones.

This is why TCP uses timestamps (Options field) to disambiguate segments with the same sequence number.

Q12. How do TCP timestamps help with wrap around?

Timestamps mark the age of segments.

If two segments have the same sequence number, the receiver uses the timestamp to identify the correct (newer) segment.

This is especially important in high-speed networks where WAT is small.

Q13. What is PAWS (Protection Against Wrapped Sequence numbers)?

A mechanism using TCP timestamps to reject old duplicate segments that have wrapped around.

If a segment’s timestamp is older than the last received timestamp, it is discarded.

Q14. What is the significance of the sequence number in the Three-Way Handshake?

Step 1 (SYN): Client sends SYN = 1, Seq = X (ISN).

Step 2 (SYN-ACK): Server sends SYN = 1, ACK = 1, Seq = Y (server ISN), Ack = X + 1.

Step 3 (ACK): Client sends ACK = 1, Seq = X + 1, Ack = Y + 1.

This synchronizes the initial sequence numbers of both sides.

Q15. What happens if the sequence number wraps around during a long-lived connection?

TCP relies on timestamps and PAWS to prevent confusion.

Without timestamps, old duplicate segments could be accepted as new data — a serious correctness issue.

This is why window scaling and timestamps are important in modern TCP.

🔹 Quick Viva Traps for Lecture 31
Trap Question	Correct Answer
“Does the sequence number count segments or bytes?”	Bytes — TCP is a byte-stream protocol.
“Is the ISN always 0?”	No — it is chosen randomly for security and correctness.
“Does SYN consume a sequence number?”	Yes — SYN consumes 1 sequence number.
“What is the sequence number of the first data byte if SYN Seq = 1000?”	1001.
“Can wrap around cause data corruption?”	Yes, if not handled with timestamps/PAWS.
“What is the formula for WAT?”	2³² / Data Rate (bytes/sec).
✅ What to Say in Viva (Lecture 31 Summary)
TCP sequence number = 32-bit, assigned per byte.

SYN and FIN consume one sequence number each.

ISN is randomly chosen and synchronized during the Three-Way Handshake.

Wrap Around Time = 2³² / Data Rate.

WAT must be greater than MSL (ideally > 2×MSL) to avoid old-segment confusion.

Timestamps + PAWS protect against wrapped sequence numbers.

