🔹 Lecture 25: Routing Algorithms & Distance Vector Routing (DVR)
Part A: Routing Algorithm Basics
Q1. What is the purpose of a routing algorithm?

To determine the routing of packets in a node — i.e., deciding the next hop for a packet destined for a particular network.

Q2. How are routing algorithms classified?

Static (Non-adaptive) Routing – routes are fixed/configured manually.

Dynamic (Adaptive) Routing – routes change based on network topology/traffic (e.g., DVR, Link State Routing).

Q3. Is Distance Vector Routing static or dynamic?

It is a dynamic routing algorithm — routing tables are updated as the network changes.

Part B: Distance Vector Routing – Core Concept
Q4. What is Distance Vector Routing (DVR)?

A dynamic routing algorithm where each router maintains a distance vector — a table of minimum distances (costs) to all known destinations and the next hop to reach them.

Q5. What does each router know in Step-01 (local knowledge)?

All routers present in the network.

The distance (cost) to its immediate neighbors only.

Its own distance to itself = 0.

Q6. What is a “distance vector”?

A 1-D array of shortest known distances from a router to every other router in the network.

