🔹 Lecture 25 (Continuation): Link State Routing & DVR Problems — Viva Q&A
Context: Lecture 25 covered Distance Vector Routing (DVR). The next logical viva topics are Link State Routing (LSR), Dijkstra’s algorithm, and the problems with DVR (Count-to-Infinity). Examiners often ask these together.

Part A: Problems with Distance Vector Routing
Q1. What is the Count-to-Infinity problem in DVR?

When a link fails, routers may slowly increment their distance to a destination instead of immediately marking it unreachable.

Routers keep believing each other that a path exists, causing the distance to climb to infinity.

This creates a routing loop and slow convergence.

Q2. Why does Count-to-Infinity happen?

A router hears from a neighbor a route that actually goes back through itself.

It has no way to know the neighbor’s path depends on it.

Example: A–B link fails; B tells A “I can reach X via you,” and A believes B.

Q3. What are the solutions to Count-to-Infinity?

Solution	How it works
Split Horizon	A router does not advertise a route back to the neighbor from which it learned it.
Poison Reverse	A router advertises the route back to that neighbor with infinite cost (instead of not advertising).
Triggered Updates	Send updates immediately when a change occurs, not waiting for the periodic timer.
Hold-down Timers	Ignore new route information for a period after a link failure to let the network stabilize.
Route Poisoning	Advertise the failed route with infinite metric to flush bad routes.
Q4. What is the difference between Split Horizon and Poison Reverse?

Split Horizon: Do not advertise the route back to the neighbor you learned it from.

Poison Reverse: Advertise it with infinity — more explicit and slightly more robust.

Part B: Link State Routing (LSR)
Q5. What is Link State Routing?

A dynamic routing algorithm where every router:

Discovers its neighbors and their costs.
Floods link-state information to all routers in the network.
Builds a complete topology map of the network.
Runs Dijkstra’s algorithm to compute shortest paths.
Q6. How does LSR differ from DVR?

Feature	DVR	LSR
Knowledge	Only neighbors	Entire topology
Information exchanged	Distance vectors	Link-state packets (LSPs)
Algorithm	Bellman-Ford	Dijkstra
Convergence	Slow	Fast
Count-to-Infinity	Yes	No
Memory/CPU	Low	High
Q7. What is a Link-State Packet (LSP)?

Contains: Router ID, list of neighbors, cost to each neighbor, sequence number, and age.

Flooded to all routers in the network.

Q8. What is flooding in LSR?

Every router sends its LSP to all other routers (not just neighbors).

Uses sequence numbers to detect old/duplicate LSPs.

Uses age field to discard stale LSPs.

Q9. Why is sequence number important in LSP?

To distinguish new LSPs from old ones.

A router accepts an LSP only if its sequence number is higher than the last one seen.

Q10. What is the role of the Age field in LSP?

Prevents old LSPs from circulating forever.

Each LSP has a maximum age (e.g., 3600 seconds); after that, it is discarded.

Part C: Dijkstra’s Algorithm (Used in LSR)
Q11. What is Dijkstra’s algorithm used for?

To compute the shortest path from a source router to all other routers in the network, given the complete topology.

Q12. What are the steps of Dijkstra’s algorithm?

Mark the source as permanent (distance = 0); all others temporary (∞).

For each neighbor of the permanent node, update the tentative distance.

Pick the temporary node with the smallest distance; make it permanent.

Repeat until all nodes are permanent.

The next hop for each destination is the first hop on the shortest path.

Q13. What is the time complexity of Dijkstra’s algorithm?

With a simple array: O(n²).

With a priority queue (heap): O((n + e) log n), where n = nodes, e = edges.

Q14. Does Dijkstra’s algorithm work with negative edge weights?

No. It assumes non-negative weights. For negative weights, use Bellman-Ford.

Q15. In LSR, where does Dijkstra’s algorithm run?

Independently at every router, using the same global topology map.

Each router computes its own shortest-path tree.

Part D: Comparison & Viva Traps
Q16. Which converges faster — DVR or LSR?

LSR converges faster because every router has the complete topology and runs Dijkstra immediately after flooding.

Q17. Which uses more memory — DVR or LSR?

LSR uses more memory (stores the entire topology).

DVR stores only a distance vector.

Q18. Which is more scalable — DVR or LSR?

DVR is simpler and scales better for small networks.

LSR is better for large networks (e.g., OSPF uses LSR).

Q19. What protocol uses DVR?

RIP (Routing Information Protocol) — uses hop count as metric (max 15 hops).

Q20. What protocol uses LSR?

OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) — uses Dijkstra’s algorithm.

Q21. Can DVR and LSR coexist in the same network?

Yes — different autonomous systems or areas can use different routing protocols (e.g., RIP at the edge, OSPF in the core).

Part E: Quick Viva One-Liners
Count-to-Infinity is the classic DVR problem.

Split Horizon, Poison Reverse, Triggered Updates fix it.

LSR = complete topology + Dijkstra.

DVR = neighbor knowledge + Bellman-Ford.

LSP = Router ID + neighbors + cost + sequence number + age.

Flooding distributes LSPs to all routers.

Sequence number prevents old LSPs from being accepted.

Age field discards stale LSPs.

RIP uses DVR; OSPF uses LSR.

Dijkstra fails with negative weights — use Bellman-Ford.

