🔹 Lecture 26 (Expected): Link State Routing & Dijkstra’s Algorithm — Viva Q&A
Part A: Link State Routing — Core Concepts
Q1. What is Link State Routing (LSR)?

A dynamic routing algorithm where every router:

Discovers its direct neighbors and the cost to each.
Floods link-state information to all routers in the network.
Builds a complete topology map (Link State Database).
Runs Dijkstra’s algorithm to compute shortest paths.
Q2. What are the four main steps of LSR?

Neighbor discovery — send “Hello” packets to find neighbors.

Flooding — distribute Link State Packets (LSPs) to all routers.

Topology construction — each router builds the full network map.

Shortest path computation — run Dijkstra’s algorithm.

Q3. What is a Link State Packet (LSP)?

Contains:

Router ID (unique identifier)

List of neighbors and cost to each

Sequence number (to detect old/duplicate LSPs)

Age field (to discard stale LSPs)

Q4. Why is a sequence number needed in LSP?

To distinguish new LSPs from old ones.

A router accepts an LSP only if its sequence number is higher than the last one seen from that router.

Prevents duplicate or stale LSPs from being accepted.

Q5. What is the role of the Age field in LSP?

Prevents old LSPs from circulating forever.

Each LSP has a maximum age (e.g., 3600 seconds); after that, it is discarded.

The age is decremented as the LSP travels through the network.

Q6. What is flooding in LSR?

Every router sends its LSP to all other routers (not just neighbors).

Uses sequence numbers and age to avoid infinite flooding.

Ensures every router has the same topology map.

Q7. What are the problems with flooding?

Duplicate LSPs — solved by sequence numbers.

Old LSPs — solved by age field.

Excessive traffic — controlled by limiting LSP generation frequency.

Part B: Dijkstra’s Algorithm (Used in LSR)
Q8. What is Dijkstra’s algorithm used for in LSR?

To compute the shortest path from the source router to all other routers, given the complete topology map.

Q9. What are the steps of Dijkstra’s algorithm?

Mark the source as permanent (distance = 0); all others temporary (∞).

For each neighbor of the permanent node, update the tentative distance.

Pick the temporary node with the smallest distance; make it permanent.

Repeat until all nodes are permanent.

The next hop for each destination is the first hop on the shortest path.

Q10. What is the time complexity of Dijkstra’s algorithm?

With a simple array: O(n²).

With a priority queue (heap): O((n + e) log n), where n = nodes, e = edges.

Q11. Does Dijkstra’s algorithm work with negative edge weights?

No. It assumes non-negative weights. For negative weights, use Bellman-Ford.

Q12. In LSR, where does Dijkstra’s algorithm run?

Independently at every router, using the same global topology map.

Each router computes its own shortest-path tree.

Part C: Comparison — DVR vs LSR
Feature	DVR	LSR
Knowledge	Only neighbors	Entire topology
Information exchanged	Distance vectors	Link-state packets (LSPs)
Algorithm	Bellman-Ford	Dijkstra
Convergence	Slow	Fast
Count-to-Infinity	Yes	No
Memory/CPU	Low	High
Protocol example	RIP	OSPF
Q13. Which converges faster — DVR or LSR?

LSR converges faster because every router has the complete topology and runs Dijkstra immediately after flooding.

Q14. Which uses more memory — DVR or LSR?

LSR uses more memory (stores the entire topology).

DVR stores only a distance vector.

Q15. Which is more scalable — DVR or LSR?

DVR is simpler and scales better for small networks.

LSR is better for large networks (e.g., OSPF uses LSR).

Part D: Routing Protocols — RIP vs OSPF
Q16. What protocol uses DVR?

RIP (Routing Information Protocol) — uses hop count as metric (max 15 hops).

Q17. What protocol uses LSR?

OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) — uses Dijkstra’s algorithm.

Q18. What is the metric used in RIP?

Hop count — maximum 15 hops; 16 means unreachable.

Q19. What is the metric used in OSPF?

Cost (usually based on bandwidth; inversely proportional to link bandwidth).

Q20. What is the difference between RIP and OSPF?

Feature	RIP	OSPF
Algorithm	DVR	LSR
Metric	Hop count	Cost (bandwidth)
Max hops	15	No limit
Convergence	Slow	Fast
Scalability	Small networks	Large networks
Protocol type	Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP)	Interior Gateway Protocol (IGP)
Q21. What is an Autonomous System (AS)?

A group of networks under a single administrative domain.

Routing inside an AS uses IGP (e.g., RIP, OSPF).

Routing between ASes uses EGP (e.g., BGP).

Q22. What is the difference between IGP and EGP?

IGP (Interior Gateway Protocol): Routing within an AS (RIP, OSPF).

EGP (Exterior Gateway Protocol): Routing between ASes (BGP).

Part E: Hierarchical Routing
Q23. What is hierarchical routing?

Dividing the network into regions/areas to reduce routing table size and update traffic.

Each router knows:

Detailed topology of its own area.

Summary information about other areas.

Q24. Why is hierarchical routing needed?

Scalability — flat routing doesn’t scale to millions of routers.

Reduced table size — routers store less information.

Less update traffic — changes are contained within an area.

Q25. How does OSPF use hierarchy?

OSPF divides the AS into areas (Area 0 = backbone area).

Area Border Routers (ABRs) connect areas and summarize routes.

Part F: Quick Viva One-Liners
LSR = complete topology + Dijkstra.

LSP = Router ID + neighbors + cost + sequence number + age.

Flooding distributes LSPs to all routers.

Sequence number prevents old LSPs from being accepted.

Age field discards stale LSPs.

RIP uses DVR; OSPF uses LSR.

IGP = within AS; EGP = between ASes.

Hierarchical routing = areas/regions for scalability.

Dijkstra fails with negative weights — use Bellman-Ford.

