Example: If reference bandwidth = 100 Mbps, a 10 Mbps link has cost = 10.

Q4. What is an OSPF Area?

A logical grouping of routers within an AS.

OSPF divides the AS into areas to reduce routing table size and update traffic.

Area 0 is the backbone area — all other areas must connect to it.

Q5. What is an Area Border Router (ABR)?

A router that connects two or more OSPF areas.

It summarizes topology information from one area and advertises it to another.

Q6. What is an Autonomous System Boundary Router (ASBR)?

A router that connects an OSPF AS to another AS (e.g., runs BGP).

It injects external routes into OSPF.

Q7. What are the types of OSPF routers?

Router Type	Role
Internal Router	All interfaces in the same area
Area Border Router (ABR)	Connects multiple areas
Backbone Router	Has an interface in Area 0
ASBR	Connects to another AS
Q8. What are OSPF Link State Advertisements (LSAs)?

Type 1 (Router LSA): Generated by every router; describes its links.

Type 2 (Network LSA): Generated by DR; describes multi-access networks.

Type 3 (Summary LSA): Generated by ABRs; summarizes routes between areas.

Type 4 (ASBR Summary LSA): Generated by ABRs; advertises ASBR location.

Type 5 (External LSA): Generated by ASBRs; describes external routes.

Q9. What is a Designated Router (DR) in OSPF?

Elected on multi-access networks (e.g., Ethernet) to reduce flooding.

All routers send LSAs to the DR; the DR floods to others.

Backup DR (BDR) is also elected.

Q10. How is a DR elected in OSPF?

Based on priority (highest wins); tie-breaker is Router ID (highest wins).

Priority 0 means the router is not eligible.

Part B: BGP (Border Gateway Protocol)
Q11. What is BGP?

Border Gateway Protocol — the Exterior Gateway Protocol (EGP) used for routing between Autonomous Systems (ASes) on the Internet.

It is a path-vector protocol.

Q12. What is a path-vector protocol?

Each route is advertised with the full AS path (list of ASes traversed).

Loop prevention: A router rejects a route if its own AS appears in the AS path.

Q13. What are the two types of BGP?

eBGP (External BGP): Between different ASes.

iBGP (Internal BGP): Between routers within the same AS.

Q14. What is the BGP metric?

BGP uses path attributes (not a simple metric):

AS_PATH (shortest path preferred)

LOCAL_PREF (higher preferred)

MULTI_EXIT_DISC (MED) (lower preferred)

NEXT_HOP

ORIGIN

Q15. Why is BGP called a “path-vector” protocol?

Because it advertises the entire AS path to a destination, not just a distance.

This allows loop detection and policy-based routing.

Q16. What is the difference between IGP and EGP?

Feature	IGP	EGP
Scope	Within an AS	Between ASes
Examples	RIP, OSPF	BGP
Metric	Hop count, cost	Path attributes
Focus	Shortest path	Policy and reachability
Q17. What is a “BGP speaker”?

A router that runs BGP and exchanges routing information with other BGP speakers.

Q18. What is a “BGP peer” or “neighbor”?

Two BGP speakers that have established a TCP connection (port 179) to exchange routes.

Part C: Hierarchical Routing & Route Aggregation
Q19. Why is hierarchical routing needed?

Scalability — flat routing doesn’t scale to millions of routers.

Reduced table size — routers store less information.

Less update traffic — changes are contained within an area.

Q20. What is route aggregation (summarization)?

Combining multiple specific routes into a single summary route.

Example: 192.168.0.0/24, 192.168.1.0/24 → 192.168.0.0/23.

Q21. What is CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing)?

Allows variable-length subnet masks (VLSM).

Enables route aggregation by using prefixes (e.g., /21, /23).

Replaced the old class-based addressing (A, B, C).

Q22. What is the longest prefix match rule?

When multiple routes match a destination, the router chooses the one with the longest prefix (most specific match).

Example: 192.168.1.0/24 is preferred over 192.168.0.0/16 for 192.168.1.5.

Part D: Quick Viva One-Liners
OSPF = Link State + Dijkstra + Areas.

Area 0 = backbone area.

ABR connects areas; ASBR connects to other ASes.

DR/BDR reduce flooding on multi-access networks.

BGP = path-vector protocol for inter-AS routing.

eBGP = between ASes; iBGP = within an AS.

AS_PATH = loop prevention in BGP.

CIDR enables route aggregation.

Longest prefix match = most specific route wins.

IGP = within AS (RIP, OSPF); EGP = between ASes (BGP).

