Part B: CSMA/CD — Core Concepts
Q7. What does CSMA/CD stand for?

Carrier Sense Multiple Access / Collision Detection.

Q8. What is the basic idea of CSMA/CD?

A station senses the carrier before transmitting.

If free → transmit; if busy → wait.

If a collision occurs, the station detects it, sends a jam signal, waits a random back-off time, and retries.

Q9. What is the “Carrier Sense” step?

Any station willing to transmit senses the carrier.

If free → starts transmitting; otherwise → waits.

Limitation: Each station can sense the carrier only at its point of contact, not the entire carrier.

So, a station may sense the carrier as free even when it is actually not → collision.

Q10. Explain the collision scenario with an example.

Station A senses carrier free at 10:00 am and starts transmitting.

Propagation delay = 1 hour.

At 10:59:59, station D senses the carrier (still free at its point) and starts transmitting.

Collision occurs at 11:00 am.

The collided signal travels back to A in 1 hour.

For A to detect the collision, it must still be transmitting → T_t ≥ 2T_p.

