Part C: Important Notes & Capture Effect
Q13. Note-1: What happens with each successive collision?

Back-off time increases exponentially.

Collision probability decreases exponentially.

Q14. Note-2: Why is it called “Binary Exponential Back-off”?

It works for two stations (binary).

Back-off time increases exponentially; collision probability decreases exponentially.

Q15. Note-3: What is the Capture Effect?

Definition: If a particular station wins a collision once, its probability of winning successive collisions increases exponentially.

Why? The winning station's packet is successfully transmitted, so its next packet starts with collision number = 1 (small range).

The losing station's packet keeps colliding, so its collision number grows → its range grows → it waits longer → the winner keeps winning.

Disadvantage: Unfairness — one station can dominate the channel.

