Part D: Ethernet — Characteristics
Q13. What is Ethernet?

A standard LAN technology for building wired LANs.

Defined under IEEE 802.3.

Q14. What topology does Ethernet use?

Bus topology — all stations connect to a single half-duplex shared link.

Q15. What access control method does Ethernet use?

CSMA/CD (Carrier Sense Multiple Access / Collision Detection).

Q16. What encoding technique does Ethernet use?

Manchester encoding — converts data bits into signals.

Q17. What are the bandwidths for different Ethernet variants?

Variant	Bandwidth
Normal Ethernet	10 Mbps
Fast Ethernet	100 Mbps
Gigabit Ethernet	1 Gbps
Q18. What are the standard LAN technologies?

Ethernet (IEEE 802.3)

Token Ring (IEEE 802.5)

Q19. What are the advantages of Ethernet?

Simple to understand and implement.

Easy maintenance.

Cheap.

Q20. What are the limitations of Ethernet?

Cannot be used for real-time applications (high collision probability causes delay).

Cannot be used for interactive applications (e.g., chat) — minimum data field is 46 bytes, which is too large for tiny chat messages.

No priority mechanism — client-server applications need server priority, which Ethernet lacks.

Token Ring overcomes these limitations.

Part E: Ethernet Frame Format (IEEE 802.3)
Q21. What is the IEEE 802.3 Ethernet frame format?

Field	Size	Purpose
Preamble	7 bytes	Alternating 0s and 1s; alerts stations and establishes bit synchronization
Start Frame Delimiter (SFD)	1 byte	Always 10101011; last two bits 11 mark the beginning of the frame
Destination Address	6 bytes	MAC address of the destination
Source Address	6 bytes	MAC address of the source
Length	2 bytes	Length of the data field (in bytes)
Data (Payload)	46–1500 bytes	Actual data
Frame Check Sequence (CRC)	4 bytes	CRC code for error detection
Q22. What is the total frame size range (from Destination Address to CRC)?

Minimum: 64 bytes

Maximum: 1518 bytes

Including Preamble + SFD: 72 bytes (min) to 1526 bytes (max)

Q23. What is the size of the Preamble and SFD?

Preamble = 7 bytes; SFD = 1 byte.

Some texts combine them as an 8-byte preamble — both are correct.

Q24. Who adds the Preamble and SFD?

The physical layer — they represent the physical layer header.

Q25. What is the value of the SFD?

10101011 — the last two bits 11 indicate the end of SFD and the beginning of the frame.

Q26. What is the size of the Destination and Source Address fields?

Both are 6 bytes (48 bits) — these are MAC addresses.

Q27. Why is the Length field needed?

Because Ethernet uses variable-sized frames — the receiver needs to know where the data ends.

